# Target C — Section 126: the pure-tooth one-cell endpoint has an exact train-shear normal form

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** proof-only research candidate stacked on canonical audit.46 and proof-only Section 125.  
**Dependencies:** canonical Sections 57, 62, 63, 67, 82, 123; the finite stopped-train theorem DT; the exact outer-to-outer train shear argument ZGS.

Section 125 isolates the sharp residual regression in the full-core route: augmented side-faces can be almost entirely two-edge triangular teeth. This section classifies the **exact pure endpoint** of that regression.

Assume a finite post-ZF C-II source has exactly one unloaded positive-contact component `I`, its attachment set is the entire loaded set `A=B`, and, in the canonical Section-123 augmented cell `J^+`,

\[
\boxed{T_\triangle=q,\qquad Q^+=0,}
\tag{126.1}
\]

where `q=|B|`, `T_triangle` is the augmented triangular-face count, and `Q^+` is the augmented degree-above-four defect.

Then:

1. every attachment-polygon side closes a two-edge outer contact route into a triangular tooth;
2. the full positive contact core has
   \[
   \boxed{e_{BB}=k=d_H=s_Z=Q=0,\qquad h=q;}
   \tag{126.2}
   \]
   hence the loaded diameter graph is connected odd-unicyclic;
3. every bounded contact face is a strict unit rhombus and the global stopped-train decomposition has exactly
   \[
   \boxed{T_{train}=q}
   \tag{126.3}
   \]
   trains, all stopped only at the actual outer face;
4. the contact rigidity matrix has independent rows and its infinitesimal contact-flex space modulo Euclidean motions has dimension exactly `q-1`;
5. train angular velocities give complete coordinates on the contact-flex space: modulo translations,
   \[
   \boxed{\mathcal F_C/\mathrm{Trans}\cong\mathbb R^q,}
   \tag{126.4}
   \]
   with the common vector `1` representing Euclidean rotation; and
6. in these exact train coordinates the `q` active diameter derivatives form a square matrix `A_train` satisfying
   \[
   \boxed{
   \operatorname{rank}A_{train}=q-1,\qquad
   \ker A_{train}=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf1\},
   }
   \tag{126.5}
   \]
   \[
   \boxed{
   \ker A_{train}^{T}=\operatorname{span}\{\beta\},
   \qquad \beta_h>0\text{ for every diameter edge }h.
   }
   \tag{126.6}
   \]

Thus the exact pure-tooth endpoint is not an arbitrary near-rhombic disk. It is a **minimally diameter-pinned train-shear system**: there are exactly as many stopped trains as active diameter edges, the only right-kernel direction is common rotation, and the only left-kernel direction is the strictly positive KKT diameter weight vector.

This is not yet a contradiction. It reduces exact pure-tooth closure to a finite sign/topology problem for the train-cut/diameter-thrackle derivative matrix.

---

## 126.1 The augmented ledger kills every loaded/contact-topology surplus

There is exactly one unloaded component and its attachment set is all of `B`, so the canonical tile defect is

\[
d_{tile}=q-2-(q-2)=0.
\tag{126.7}
\]

Canonical Section 123 gives

\[
e_{BB}-k
={1\over2}(q-T_\triangle+Q^+)+d_{tile}.
\tag{126.8}
\]

Under (126.1),

\[
\boxed{e_{BB}-k=0.}
\tag{126.9}
\]

Canonical Section 63 gives the stronger exact decomposition

\[
e_{BB}=Q+2k+u_B+c_B,
\qquad Q,k,u_B,c_B\ge0.
\tag{126.10}
\]

Subtract `k` and use (126.9):

\[
0=Q+k+u_B+c_B.
\]

Hence every term vanishes:

\[
\boxed{Q=k=u_B=c_B=0,\qquad e_{BB}=0.}
\tag{126.11}
\]

Since

\[
k=d_H+s_Z,
\qquad d_H,s_Z\ge0,
\]

we obtain

\[
\boxed{d_H=s_Z=0.}
\tag{126.12}
\]

By definition `d_H=q-h`, so

\[
\boxed{h=q.}
\tag{126.13}
\]

The canonical Woodall split identifies `d_H=0` exactly with the connected odd-unicyclic loaded diameter graph. This proves (126.2).

---

## 126.2 The contact core is a no-defect rhombic graph with `q` stopped trains

Because `e_BB=0`, the full positive contact core is exactly the graph consisting of

- the internal contacts of `I`; and
- the attachment contacts from `I` to `B`.

There is no additional loaded-loaded contact edge.

Canonical Section 67 gives the global post-ZF face identity

\[
b+Q=2(q-k).
\tag{126.14}
\]

Using `Q=k=0`,

\[
\boxed{b=2q.}
\tag{126.15}
\]

Every bounded contact face is triangle-free and has degree at least four. Since the total bounded degree excess `Q` is zero, every bounded face has degree exactly four. Canonical convex-face geometry therefore makes every bounded face a strict unit rhombus.

Now apply the finite stopped-train theorem DT to the full contact core. With `f=0` bounded nonquadrilateral faces and `Q=0`, DT gives

\[
T_{train}
={b+Q\over2}+2f
={2q\over2}
=q.
\tag{126.16}
\]

There is no bounded exceptional face at which a train can stop. Hence every non-singleton train has both physical endpoints on the actual global outer face. A singleton stopped train is a contact edge incident to no quadrilateral; with no bounded nonquadrilateral face available, both sides are outer-face occurrences, so that edge is a bridge of the contact graph.

Thus every one of the `q` stopped trains is an outer-to-outer contact cut in the following sense:

- for a non-singleton train, the ZGS midpoint-curve/Jordan-closure argument makes its parallel contact edges exactly one global cutset;
- for a singleton train, the one edge is itself a bridge cutset.

In either case there is an exact one-parameter contact-preserving shear that rotates precisely the contact edges of that train and translates one shore rigidly. This will be used below.

---

## 126.3 Contact rows are independent

Let `m` be the number of vertices in the nonisolated positive-contact core. Canonical Section 57 gives mixed active rigidity rank

\[
\operatorname{rank}R_{C\cup H}=2m-3.
\tag{126.17}
\]

The exact post-ZF surplus is

\[
s_Z=e+h-(2m-2).
\tag{126.18}
\]

Since `s_Z=0` and `h=q`,

\[
\boxed{e=2m-2-q.}
\tag{126.19}
\]

The mixed row set has

\[
e+h=2m-2
\]

rows and rank `2m-3`, hence its left kernel is one-dimensional. The relative-interior KKT stress spans that left kernel and has strictly positive diameter coordinates `beta_h`.

Suppose the contact rigidity matrix `R_C` had a nonzero row dependence `gamma`. Extending `gamma` by zero on every diameter row would give a mixed row dependence linearly independent of the KKT stress, because its diameter coordinates vanish while the KKT diameter coordinates are all positive. This contradicts one-dimensional mixed left nullity.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}R_C=e.}
\tag{126.20}
\]

The infinitesimal contact-flex space `F_C=ker R_C` has dimension

\[
\dim\mathcal F_C
=2m-e
=q+2.
\tag{126.21}
\]

After quotienting the three Euclidean infinitesimal motions,

\[
\boxed{
\dim(\mathcal F_C/\mathrm{Euc})=q-1.
}
\tag{126.22}
\]

---

## 126.4 Train angular velocity is well-defined on every contact flex

Fix one stopped train `tau`. All contact edges belonging to `tau` are parallel in the realized source.

For an infinitesimal contact flex `v`, a unit contact edge with vector `u` has derivative perpendicular to `u`, so write its edge-vector derivative as

\[
\dot u=\omega Ju.
\]

Across one strict rhombus, let `u,u` be one pair of opposite side vectors and `w,w` the other pair, with infinitesimal angular rates `omega_1,omega_3` on the opposite `u` sides and `eta_2,eta_4` on the opposite `w` sides. Differentiating the parallelogram closure gives

\[
(\omega_1-\omega_3)Ju+(\eta_2-\eta_4)Jw=0.
\]

The two rhombus directions are linearly independent, so

\[
\omega_1=\omega_3,
\qquad
\eta_2=\eta_4.
\tag{126.23}
\]

Therefore angular rate propagates unchanged across opposite sides of every rhombus. It is constant on each stopped train, including singleton trains. Denote it by `theta_tau(v)`.

This defines a linear train-angle map

\[
\Phi:\mathcal F_C\longrightarrow\mathbb R^q,
\qquad
v\longmapsto(\theta_\tau(v))_\tau.
\tag{126.24}
\]

Translations lie in `ker Phi`.

If `Phi(v)=0`, every contact edge vector has zero derivative. Since the contact core is connected, velocities agree across every contact edge, so `v` is a global translation. Thus

\[
\boxed{\ker\Phi=\mathrm{Trans}.}
\tag{126.25}
\]

Using (126.21),

\[
\dim(\mathcal F_C/\mathrm{Trans})=q.
\]

The target `R^q` also has dimension `q`, so (126.25) proves the isomorphism (126.4).

Under this isomorphism an infinitesimal Euclidean rotation has the same angular rate on every contact edge, hence corresponds to

\[
\boxed{\mathbf1=(1,\ldots,1).}
\tag{126.26}
\]

Consequently

\[
\mathcal F_C/\mathrm{Euc}
\cong
\mathbb R^q/\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf1\}.
\tag{126.27}
\]

---

## 126.5 The exact train shears are the coordinate basis

For each non-singleton stopped train `tau`, the outer-to-outer ZGS shear rotates every cut contact edge of `tau` by parameter angle `theta` while every contact internal to either shore is unchanged. Its infinitesimal train-angle vector is therefore the standard basis vector `e_tau`.

For a singleton bridge train, translate one bridge shore by

\[
R_\theta u-u
\]

where `u` is the bridge contact vector. This rotates that bridge contact exactly while preserving every same-shore contact. Its infinitesimal train-angle vector is again `e_tau`.

Thus the `q` exact finite train shears map to the standard basis of `R^q`. They form a basis of `F_C/Trans`; their common sum differs from Euclidean rotation only by a translation.

This is stronger than merely counting `q` train paths: every infinitesimal contact flex is, modulo translation, a unique linear combination of exact one-train shear velocities.

---

## 126.6 The diameter derivative matrix is square and minimally pinned

Use train angular coordinates `theta in R^q`. For every active diameter edge `h=xy`, let

\[
F_h(\theta)
:=\frac12\frac d{dt}
|x(t)-y(t)|^2\Big|_{t=0}
\]

under the infinitesimal contact flex with train coordinate `theta`. Let `A_train` be the `q times q` matrix whose `h`-th row is this linear functional.

There are `q` rows because `h=q` by (126.13).

A common train rotation `theta=c\mathbf1` is Euclidean rotation modulo translation, so every diameter first variation vanishes. Hence

\[
A_{train}\mathbf1=0.
\tag{126.28}
\]

Canonical mixed rigidity says the only contact flexes with zero first variation on **all** active diameter rows are Euclidean. In train coordinates this says

\[
\ker A_{train}=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf1\}.
\tag{126.29}
\]

Therefore

\[
\operatorname{rank}A_{train}=q-1.
\]

Finally restrict the normalized KKT stationarity equation to the contact-flex space. Contact rows vanish identically on every contact flex, so the positive diameter weights satisfy

\[
\boxed{\beta^TA_{train}=0.}
\tag{126.30}
\]

with every `beta_h>0`. Since the matrix rank is `q-1`, its left kernel is one-dimensional, proving (126.6).

Equivalently, after any deletion of one train coordinate and any quotient by common rotation, the diameter gradients form a minimally positive spanning family: exactly one positive dependence remains.

---

## 126.7 Exact next obstruction

The pure-tooth endpoint is now reduced to the following finite matrix problem.

Every train is a circular contact cut of the actual outer face. A diameter derivative is nonzero exactly when its two endpoints lie on opposite shores of that cut; its sign and magnitude are the oriented crossing derivative

\[
(p_x-p_y)\cdot J u_\tau
\]

with the shore orientation of the train cut. The active diameter edges form a straight-line convex thrackle whose graph is connected odd-unicyclic.

The required matrix therefore has simultaneously:

1. circular-split support columns coming from the `q` stopped train cuts;
2. row supports and signs coming from the `q` pairwise-intersecting diameter chords;
3. `A_train 1=0`;
4. rank `q-1`; and
5. a strictly positive left kernel vector `beta`.

A proof that this signed circular-split matrix class cannot satisfy all five properties would eliminate the exact pure-tooth endpoint. The existing two-edge cusp regression shows that arbitrary sparse split matrices can satisfy local positive-span conditions, so the missing theorem must use the **global odd-unicyclic diameter-thrackle plus the complete train-cut system**, not one shear at a time.

This section does not assert that such a matrix is impossible.

---

## 126.8 Scope

- Contact-isolated source points remain in the full source. Section 126 concerns the nonisolated positive-contact core and does not delete isolates or add their coordinates to the Section-57 affine kernel.
- The one-cell hypothesis is exact: there is exactly one unloaded positive-contact component and its attachment set is all loaded vertices.
- The pure-tooth hypothesis is exact: `T_triangle=q` and `Q^+=0` in the canonical augmented graph.
- Singleton stopped trains are treated as bridge shears directly; no rhombus is invented around them.
- No theorem-state authority changes. Audit.46 remains canonical through Section 123; Sections 124--126 are proof-only.

**Disposition:** exact endpoint normal form. C-II and Level I remain open.
